course: dictionary
lesson: 14680
title: Big words for being in charge
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word preside means to be in charge of something, like a meeting, a court or an event. Context helps you find it. «The judge presided over the trial.» A trial needs someone in charge, so presided must mean was in charge of.
explain: Preside can also mean to be in control of a time or a situation. «He presided over a time of peace.» Peace does not happen by itself, so he was the person in control during it. Ask yourself: who is in charge here? That question usually gives you the meaning.
explain: Preside is a verb, so it changes: preside, presides, presided, presiding. Use presides for now, and presided for the past. «The chairperson presides over the class council every Friday.» Presided is what you write about yesterday.
fact: Preside means to be in charge of a meeting, ceremony, court or event.
fact: «The judge presided over the trial.» means the judge was in charge of the trial.
fact: A chairperson presides over a meeting, and keeps it running in order.
fact: In Alice in Wonderland, the King of Hearts presides over the trial of the Knave of Hearts.
fact: A priest or minister may preside at communion in church.
exercise: «The judge presided over the trial.» What does presided mean here? | *Was in charge of the trial | Ran out of the court | Was found guilty | Sat quietly at the back and ate sweets
exercise: «The chairperson presided over the meeting.» Who was in charge of the meeting? | The person who made the tea | *The chairperson | The youngest child in the room | The visitor at the back
exercise: In Alice in Wonderland, who presides over the trial of the Knave of Hearts? | The Knave of Hearts | *The King of Hearts | The White Rabbit | The Queen
test: «He presided over a time of peace.» What does presided over suggest? | Forgot about the peace | Stayed asleep through it | *Was in control during a time of peace | Started a war
test: Which word means about the same as preside over? | Watched from afar | Ignored | Ran | *Led
exam: «The judge presided over the trial.» What is the judge doing? | Sleeping in the back row | Eating lunch in the court | *Being in charge of the trial and keeping order | Being tried for a crime
